overhead, but since the question says to ignore overhead the comparison stands. STCF also requires knowledge/estimation of remaining processing time, which RR does not.\n\n=== Is the better-average-response policy best for every objective? ===\nNo. A smaller average response time (or switching fraction) alone does not establish that a policy is best for every objective. In this example STCF dominates on both averages, but that is a property of this workload, not a universal law. Metrics measure different things: early access to the processor does not imply early completion, and a policy can satisfy one constraint while losing on another — e.g., when context-switch overhead matters, RR's or STCF's preemptions raise switching cost; when long-job completion or fairness/anti-starvation is the goal, preemptive shortest-job policies can delay long jobs (here A runs to 10 under both, but with more short jobs A could be pushed further); and STCF depends on accurate knowledge of future service times. Policy choice should follow the stated workload and requirement, not a single metric.",
